# Why M9 and M11 are both here, and are not the same test twice: the buffer has
# to say YES for a sleeve and NO for a skirt, and one mutation can only prove
# one of those. Marking nothing arm-driven passes the skirt test; marking
# everything arm-driven passes nothing, and it is the pair that pins the answer
# rather than either half.
#
# M10 is the gate's own condition and M9/M11 are the buffer it reads, which is
# why they are three mutations and not one: with the buffer stuck at all-false
# the gate condition is vacuously true and its own test still goes green.

M16 partmap says an arm drives the skirt too: exit 1, tests ran=True, 1 red
    FAIL: test_it_drops_a_hand_resting_against_a_skirt_on_the_shipped_body (pierce_test.ArmDrivenCloth)

# M16 is the one that pins the loosening on the body every visitor sees. The
# arm condition takes `Outfit_Bottom` off Milfy's worst-part list entirely --
# 97 pixels to 0 at modelPose t=4.7s, and the body's worst clip ratio from 0.69
# to 0.47 -- and a change that large needs a test saying WHY those pixels may
# go. M16 declares the skirt arm-driven, which removes the justification, and
# the test reads it.
#
# THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT TEST COULD NOT FAIL. It defined the dropped pixels
# as `without & ~(~skin_arm | cloth_arm)` and then asserted that they are arm
# skin and that no arm drives their cloth -- which is that expression restated,
# true for any input. Only the `dropped_total > 50` line carried anything, so
# M16 was red on a bound, not on a reason. What the test asserts now is what the
# rule does not determine: that the garment losing the pixels is the skirt and
# only the skirt, and that the skirt goes from over 50 pixels to none. M16 is
# red on that.
